A borrower takes a $53,000 adjustable-rate loan on a 180-month level-payment schedule at 4.2% annual interest compounded monthly (a 0.35% monthly rate). Immediately after the 60th monthly payment, the rate resets to 7.2% annual (a 0.6% monthly rate), and the remaining balance is re-amortized over the remaining 120 months with a new level payment starting in month 61. Each month interest accrues on the outstanding balance first, then the payment is applied to principal. Work to exact cents: round each level payment and each month's interest to the nearest cent; the final payment is whatever amount clears the remaining balance. What is the new monthly payment after the reset? Give your answer in dollars to the nearest cent. Reply with only the number.